d costs and conditional Saved values are estimates. Food .52 and furniture/bedding .61 are announced approximate category means; other goods .59 is a prior from published grade odds and cost-range midpoints. Seller unit, grade and purchase-time surge may differ. Never treat these as verified costs, floor prices or guaranteed score improvements."}, "selection_policy": {"mode": "model_choice", "forced_candidate_id": null, "note": "In exploration the harness fixes the candidate; choose its opening price. The buyer may still negotiate or walk away later. All checkout guards apply."}, "objective": "Choose one available SKU and shop, plus a first offer. Maximize likely positive normalized Saved contribution and valid settlement chance, not absolute currency surplus. Compare the share of seller margin saved, not the discount as a share of list price. The saved-at-cap candidate order is conditional, not an actual quote or guaranteed deal. Use the recent exact-SKU paid median and suggested maximum where available. same_shop_sku samples describe this seller; same_sku samples pool other sellers. saved_if_usable_at_reference is a historical comparison, not a current quote. Separately, saved_reference is the largest whole price that would improve the displayed aggregate Saved if this purchase is usable and the cost scenario is correct. Costs are private; category averages are uncertain estimates. If official_score_status is behind_local_paid_evidence, the score snapshot is stale and its price line is only a soft reference. Treat this as negotiation guidance and weigh settlement likelihood and current quality. Check current shelf life, grade and warranty before comparing these historical prices. routing_evidence summarizes recent comparable completed orders and smoothed timing/probability estimates. Sparse samples use explicit priors. Receipts are not payments; outcomes depend on offers and unit conditions. Prefer expected usable margin savings per attempt, then timing that fits 